<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Panjikent</h1><p class="st-meta">500 CE – 722 CE (approx.) · capital Panjikent</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Sogdian town-state whose excavated houses and murals give the most vivid picture of everyday life on the early medieval Silk Road.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panjikent">Panjikent</a>, on the Zarafshan River east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, was founded in the 5th century and grew into a walled town of merchants and landowners. It was one of several principalities of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-samarkand">Sogdiana</a>, often under the influence of the Principality of Samarkand, and within the orbit of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Excavations since 1946 have revealed a citadel, two temples and streets of two-storey houses whose reception halls were covered in wall paintings of epics, gods and banquets, including scenes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rustam</a> cycle. Its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divashtich">Divashtich</a> styled himself king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-samarkand">Sogdiana</a> and ikhshid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. Its archive of documents on leather and wood was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mugh">Mount Mugh</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divashtich">Divashtich</a> resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad</a> advance, was captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mugh">Mount Mugh</a> in 722 and crucified. The town declined and was abandoned in the late 8th century, which preserved its ruins for archaeologists.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 500 CE – 722 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:69.65%;width:4.42%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 CE – 722 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Panjikent</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Divashtich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sogdian Zoroastrianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sogdian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Panch, Panjakent</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=611&amp;focus=principality-of-panjikent" aria-label="Principality of Panjikent on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6487 805 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Panjikent lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6807 1061h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6807 1061h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Tajikistan. <a href="/atlas?year=611&amp;focus=principality-of-panjikent">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

